{
  "concept": "Asymptotic notations Θ, O, and Ω express that some constant multiple of g(n) bounds f(n) from both sides, above only, or below only, for all n beyond a threshold n0.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "panel (a): L-shaped axes, horizontal axis tip labeled n, unmarked vertical axis",
    "panel (a): upper curve labeled c2 g(n)",
    "panel (a): lower curve labeled c1 g(n)",
    "panel (a): middle curve labeled f(n), weaving and crossing near the left edge before settling between the other two",
    "panel (a): dashed vertical line from the crossing region down to axis tick labeled n0",
    "panel (a): caption f(n) = Θ(g(n)) and panel tag (a)",
    "panel (a): shaded corridor between c1 g(n) and c2 g(n) for n ≥ n0 (permitted region)",
    "panel (b): L-shaped axes, horizontal axis tip labeled n, unmarked vertical axis",
    "panel (b): upper curve labeled c g(n)",
    "panel (b): lower curve labeled f(n), oscillating sharply at small n, meeting c g(n) at n0, then staying beneath it",
    "panel (b): dashed vertical line at axis tick labeled n0",
    "panel (b): caption f(n) = O(g(n)) and panel tag (b)",
    "panel (b): shaded region below c g(n) for n ≥ n0 (permitted region)",
    "panel (c): L-shaped axes, horizontal axis tip labeled n, unmarked vertical axis",
    "panel (c): upper curve labeled f(n)",
    "panel (c): lower curve labeled c g(n)",
    "panel (c): curves touching at the dashed vertical line over axis tick n0",
    "panel (c): caption f(n) = Ω(g(n)) and panel tag (c)",
    "panel (c): shaded region above c g(n) for n ≥ n0 (permitted region)",
    "red violation markers on segments of f(n) that escape the permitted region to the right of n0",
    "visual emphasis (border/brightness) on the active panel, with inactive panels dimmed"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thresholdN0",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Threshold n0",
      "range": [0, 100, 1],
      "default": 30,
      "teaches": "Asymptotic bounds only have to hold for n ≥ n0, so the threshold can be placed past any finite region of messy early behavior — wiggles before n0 never disqualify the bound"
    },
    {
      "id": "constantMultiplier",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Constant multiplier c",
      "range": [0.5, 2.5, 0.1],
      "default": 1.5,
      "teaches": "The bound curve is a constant rescaling of g(n): in Θ the corridor c1 g(n) to c2 g(n) widens or narrows (c1 = 1/c, c2 = c), in O the ceiling c g(n) rises or falls, in Ω the floor c g(n) rises or falls — the notation only requires that some constant works, which is why constant factors are ignored"
    },
    {
      "id": "notationView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Notation",
      "options": ["Θ — two-sided", "O — upper only", "Ω — lower only"],
      "default": "Θ — two-sided",
      "teaches": "Θ constrains f(n) from both sides at once, O only caps it from above, and Ω only floors it from below — the shaded permitted region makes the one-sided versus two-sided difference visible"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Θ: a two-sided corridor",
      "narration": "In panel (a), f(n) is sandwiched between c1 g(n) and c2 g(n) for all n ≥ n0; the shaded corridor is the region f(n) must stay in once n passes the dashed line.",
      "state": { "notationView": "Θ — two-sided", "thresholdN0": 30, "constantMultiplier": 1.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push n0 into the messy region",
      "narration": "Drag n0 left and red violation marks appear where the early oscillations of f(n) escape the corridor — showing that bounds fail if the threshold is set before the function settles down.",
      "state": { "notationView": "Θ — two-sided", "thresholdN0": 8, "constantMultiplier": 1.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Constants are negotiable",
      "narration": "Widening the multiplier restores the bound even at that early n0: any sufficiently large c2 (and small c1) works, which is exactly why asymptotic notation absorbs constant factors.",
      "state": { "notationView": "Θ — two-sided", "thresholdN0": 8, "constantMultiplier": 2.4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "O: an upper ceiling only",
      "narration": "Panel (b) shades everything below c g(n): f(n) may dip far under the ceiling and still satisfy O — only escaping above it to the right of n0 counts as a violation.",
      "state": { "notationView": "O — upper only", "thresholdN0": 30, "constantMultiplier": 1.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Ω: a lower floor only",
      "narration": "Panel (c) flips the comparison: with c < 1, c g(n) is a floor and f(n) may soar arbitrarily above it — only dipping below the floor past n0 violates Ω.",
      "state": { "notationView": "Ω — lower only", "thresholdN0": 30, "constantMultiplier": 0.7 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Θ, O, and Ω each promise only that some constant multiple of g(n) bounds f(n) from the relevant side(s) for all n past some threshold n0 — constant factors and any finite amount of early misbehavior are simply ignored.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three small sketch-style graphs side by side on a white background, mirroring CLRS Figure 3.1: thin black hand-drawn curves, L-shaped axes with no numeric ticks, an arrow-tipped horizontal axis labeled n, italic serif math labels (c2 g(n), c1 g(n), f(n), c g(n)), a dashed vertical line dropping to a tick labeled n0, the caption equation centered beneath each panel, and the panel tag (a)/(b)/(c) below that. Curves rise left to right; f(n) has small wiggles near the left edge in every panel and settles into its bounded position right of the crossing point. In Θ view the slider c sets c2 = c and c1 = 1/c so the corridor scales symmetrically about g(n); in O view c scales the ceiling; in Ω view c scales the floor (use c < 1 there so the floor sits below f(n)). The active panel is full-contrast with its permitted region shaded translucent (corridor for Θ, area under the ceiling for O, area above the floor for Ω, shading applied only for n ≥ n0); inactive panels are dimmed but still visible. Wherever f(n) exits the permitted region to the right of n0, mark that segment in red. Animate dashed-line movement and shading smoothly as sliders change."
}

